1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,646
smol ram: running very large models under a hard eight gigabyte RAM cap, by shrinking what

2
00:00:05,646 --> 00:00:07,920
every token has to read from the S S D.

3
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:12,542
Kimi K three point one Pro is 1,561 gigabytes on disk.

4
00:00:12,542 --> 00:00:18,848
With eight gigabytes of RAM, almost nothing stays resident, so every token streams its weights

5
00:00:18,848 --> 00:00:19,854
from the S S D.

6
00:00:19,854 --> 00:00:26,026
Bytes on disk are close to bytes read per token, so a smaller artifact is also a faster one.

7
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:28,244
Where are the bytes?

8
00:00:28,244 --> 00:00:31,945
Routed experts, stored in M X F P four, are almost all of it.

9
00:00:31,945 --> 00:00:37,588
But each token reads 111.5 gigabytes of always active bf sixteen weights, plus 25.8 gigabytes

10
00:00:37,588 --> 00:00:42,017
of routed experts, so the dense part dominates at eight gigabytes of RAM.

11
00:00:43,020 --> 00:00:44,450
The tools work in stages.

12
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:47,595
Shard headers give the byte budget without downloading.

13
00:00:47,595 --> 00:00:49,940
A scan measures the exact lossless floor.

14
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:54,801
A layer streamed evaluation measures the quality of each lossy lever on a single GPU.

15
00:00:54,801 --> 00:00:58,347
And a planner turns it all into S S D bound tokens per second.

16
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:01,894
Lossless coding has limited room.

17
00:01:01,894 --> 00:01:09,142
A full scan of all ninety six shards puts the bit exact floor at 1,363 gigabytes, 87.3 percent

18
00:01:09,142 --> 00:01:09,913
of native.

19
00:01:09,913 --> 00:01:13,922
The F P four expert codes are nearly incompressible.

20
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:20,378
The lossy levers, measured end to end over all 93 layers on one H100, with K L divergence

21
00:01:20,378 --> 00:01:22,156
against the unmodified model.

22
00:01:22,156 --> 00:01:27,553
Pruning twenty five percent of the least routed experts saves 322 gigabytes for the same

23
00:01:27,553 --> 00:01:31,294
divergence that int four dense costs 43 gigabytes of savings.

24
00:01:31,294 --> 00:01:33,931
Pruning is the cheapest lever per gigabyte.

25
00:01:34,930 --> 00:01:40,861
Naive round to nearest quantization below four bits is too lossy on this quantization aware

26
00:01:40,861 --> 00:01:41,774
trained model.

27
00:01:41,774 --> 00:01:47,966
Combining dense int four, experts at int two and pruning gets down to 673 gigabytes, but at the

28
00:01:47,966 --> 00:01:49,204
highest divergence.

29
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:56,265
The planner estimates S S D bound tokens per second at eight gigabytes of RAM across 5 models.

30
00:01:56,265 --> 00:02:02,137
For Kimi K three point one Pro the native recipe gives 0.05 tokens per second, and the most

31
00:02:02,137 --> 00:02:08,008
aggressive recipe 0.24, at 300 gigabytes, though that recipe's quality is not yet measured.

32
00:02:09,010 --> 00:02:09,457
Caveats.

33
00:02:09,457 --> 00:02:13,308
Quality is measured on sixteen thousand tokens with naive quantizers.

34
00:02:13,308 --> 00:02:18,554
The tokens per second figures are S S D bound estimates that ignore compute, and the streaming

35
00:02:18,554 --> 00:02:20,899
runtime itself is still on the to do list.

36
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:26,258
The reports, the tools and the planner are all in the repo.

